🚴‍♂️ Project Plan: Pi Pico W Cycling Power Broadcaster
🎯 Goal

Create a Pi Pico W-based cycling power broadcaster that:

Uses a GPS module to get position, speed, and elevation in real time.

Calculates estimated cycling power using physics.

Broadcasts the power value via Bluetooth or Wi-Fi (depending on sensor support) to compatible cycling head units (e.g., Wahoo).

🧩 Key Components
1. GPS Module Interface

Connect a GPS module to Pi Pico W via UART/I2C/SPI.

Use a MicroPython or CircuitPython library to read NMEA sentences.

Extract:

Latitude / Longitude

Altitude

Timestamp

Speed (if available, otherwise compute from position delta)

Store a small buffer of recent points to compute speed, distance, and slope.

2. Power Calculation Logic

Implement a Python version of calculate_power().

Inputs:

Rider + bike weight

Rolling resistance (Crr)

Air density (ρ)

Drag area (CdA)

Gravity (g)

Formulas:

P_gravity = weight * g * v * slope
P_roll    = weight * g * Crr * v
P_aero    = 0.5 * rho * CdA * v**3
P_total   = P_gravity + P_roll + P_aero


Smooth results using a moving average over a few samples (3–7).

Speed and slope calculations:

v = distance / time

slope = elevation gain / horizontal distance

3. Bluetooth/Wi-Fi Broadcasting

Depending on the chosen protocol:

Option A: Bluetooth LE (preferred for Wahoo pairing)

Use a BLE stack for Raspberry Pi Pico W (e.g., Adafruit BLE
).

Advertise Cycling Power Service (UUID: 0x1818).

Update Power Measurement characteristic (0x2A63) every second.

Appear to Wahoo as a virtual power meter (name: PiPower).

Option B: Wi-Fi + WebSocket / HTTP

Broadcast power via Wi-Fi to a smartphone/tablet that can forward it to head units.

Less direct than BLE, but easier to debug and extend.

4. Optional Local Display

Add an OLED / TFT screen connected to the Pico W.

Show:

Current power output (watts)

Speed (km/h or mph)

Slope (%)

GPS fix quality

5. Testing and Deployment

Flash MicroPython / CircuitPython onto Pi Pico W.

Connect GPS module and optionally a display.

Run the script and verify:

GPS coordinates are updating correctly

Power calculations are reasonable

BLE advertising appears on Wahoo / other cycling computers

Test in a stationary and moving environment to validate readings.

